(Indian Polity, Percentages, and Number Patterns) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(General Awareness: Indian Constitution) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The Constituent Assembly adopted the Constitution of India on 26 November 1949. The) Tj
T*
(Constitution came into force on 26 January 1950. India celebrates 26 January as Republic) Tj
T*
(Day. The Constitution's Preamble describes India as a sovereign, socialist, secular,) Tj
T*
(democratic republic.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Fundamental Rights are set out in Part III of the Constitution. The Right to Equality is) Tj
T*
(covered by Articles 14 to 18. Article 14 guarantees equality before the law and equal) Tj
T*
(protection of the laws. The Right to Freedom is covered by Articles 19 to 22. The) Tj
T*
(Directive Principles of State Policy are in Part IV. They guide the State when it makes) Tj
T*
(laws and policies, but courts cannot enforce them directly.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The President of India is the constitutional head of the Union. The Prime Minister leads) Tj
T*
(the Council of Ministers. Parliament consists of the President, the Lok Sabha, and the) Tj
T*
(Rajya Sabha. Members of the Lok Sabha are directly elected. The Rajya Sabha is a) Tj
T*
(continuing chamber; it is not dissolved, and about one-third of its members retire every) Tj
T*
(two years.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Mathematics: Percentages) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(A percentage means a number out of 100. To convert a fraction to a percentage, multiply) Tj
T*
(it by 100. To find p percent of a quantity, multiply the quantity by p and divide by) Tj
T*
(100.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Example 1: Find 15% of 240.) Tj
T*
(15% of 240 = \(15 multiplied by 240\), divided by 100, which equals 36.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Example 2: A price of 800 rupees increases by 10%. The increase is 80 rupees, so the new) Tj
T*
(price is 880 rupees.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Example 3: A price of 500 rupees decreases by 20%. The decrease is 100 rupees, so the) Tj
T*
(new price is 400 rupees.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(For successive percentage changes, apply each change to the updated value. An increase) Tj
T*
(of 10% followed by a decrease of 10% does not return to the starting value. Starting at) Tj
T*
(100, the increase gives 110. A 10% decrease from 110 is 11, leaving 99.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Reasoning: Number Patterns) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Look for a repeated operation between neighboring terms. Check addition, subtraction,) Tj
T*
(multiplication, division, and alternating operations. Then apply the same rule to find) Tj
T*
(the next term.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Example 1: 4, 7, 10, 13. Add 3 each time. The next term is 16.) Tj
